WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.931
Secure EF Core usage is a repeatable engineering workflow.

2
00:00:03.931 --> 00:00:16.402
It starts with clear DbContext lifetime, environment-specific configuration, protected connection settings, and database permissions that match the application's real responsibilities.

3
00:00:16.402 --> 00:00:21.282
Configuration is security code when it controls how data access behaves.

4
00:00:21.282 --> 00:00:26.908
For normal data access, prefer LINQ and framework-supported parameterized patterns.

5
00:00:26.908 --> 00:00:39.447
Keep query structure under developer control, validate values for business meaning, shape responses to return only needed data, and review performance changes for security side effects.

6
00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:43.514
Safe query syntax and safe application behavior both matter.

7
00:00:43.514 --> 00:00:46.428
Enforce authorization close to data access.

8
00:00:46.428 --> 00:00:54.833
Object-level checks, tenant boundaries, global query filters, service-layer rules, and least privilege should work together.

9
00:00:54.833 --> 00:01:03.779
Avoid bypass paths where background jobs, support tools, reporting features, or administrative actions skip the normal access model.

10
00:01:03.779 --> 00:01:05.542
Model updates defensively.

11
00:01:05.542 --> 00:01:19.911
Separate API models from persistence entities when useful, control which properties each workflow can change, review relationship changes, and protect ownership, approval, pricing, tenant, role, and audit fields.

12
00:01:19.911 --> 00:01:24.655
Tracking and concurrency behavior should match the operation's intent.

13
00:01:24.655 --> 00:01:27.570
Finally, maintain the data layer over time.

14
00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:37.330
Review raw SQL, stored procedures, migrations, seed data, EF logging, auditing, dependencies, resource limits, and operational failure behavior.

15
00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:48.987
Secure EF Core development is not a one-time checklist; it is the steady practice of keeping data access understandable, bounded, and reviewable as the application evolves.
